<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tailapa II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/973">973</a>–<a href="/years/997">997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tailapa II was a feudatory of the Rashtrakuta dynasty who claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>. In 973 he overthrew the last Rashtrakuta king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karka-ii">Karka II</a>, and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. He fought the Paramaras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and around 995 captured and executed their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakpati-munja">Vakpati Munja</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii.jpg" alt="Tailapa II" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jugaari cross · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Kannada_inscription_dated_Shaka_913_(c.991_AD)_of_Kalyani_(Western)_Chalukya_King_Ahvamalla_Tailapa_II.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tailapa, also called Taila II and known by his title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-i">Ahavamalla</a>, belonged to a family of Rashtrakuta feudatories. Its records trace his descent through an unnamed son of the Chalukya crown prince Vijayaditya, whom the Vatapi Chalukya records never mention, and the differences between the genealogy of the court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranna">Ranna</a> and later inscriptions cast doubt on the claim. His grandfather Ayyana I married a Rashtrakuta princess, which probably raised the family's standing. Inscriptions of 957 and 965 show Tailapa as a subordinate of the Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>, who granted him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Tardavadi-1000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a> died around 967, the Paramara ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaka">Siyaka</a> sacked the Rashtrakuta capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a> in 972, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karka-ii">Karka II</a> came to the throne by passing over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-iv">Indra IV</a>. Supported by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> feudatory Bhillama II and the chief Baddega II, Tailapa dethroned Karka in 973 after a battle in which Karka's two ministers were killed; sources differ on whether Karka died or escaped. Tailapa occupied Manyakheta and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and the Ganga chief Marasimha, both of whom became Jain monks, and around 975 or 976 he defeated and killed Marasimha's successor Panchaladeva.</p><p>Many former Rashtrakuta feudatories, among them the Rattas of Saundatti, the Sinda ruler Pulikala and the Nolambas, came to accept his overlordship, and he married the Rashtrakuta princess Jakavve. His son Satyashraya drove the Shilahara ruler of Konkan to the sea, and his commander Barapa took the Lata region in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. A 992 inscription says Tailapa seized 150 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> after defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king. According to the 14th-century Jain scholar Merutunga, whose account may not be accurate, the Paramara king Munja defeated Tailapa six times before Tailapa captured him, made him beg door to door and had him executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tailapa died around 997 and was succeeded by his elder son Satyashraya, then by Vikramaditya V, son of his younger son Dashavarman. He kept his headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>; later Chalukya rulers ruled from Kalyani. The Kalleshvara temple complex at Bagali was completed in his reign, and some scholars date <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranna">Ranna</a>'s Gadayuddha to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tailapa II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 973 <b>Tailapa II</b>, a feudatory based in the Bijapur region who claimed descent from the Chalukyas of Badami, overthrew Khottiga’s nephew Karka II and proclaimed the Western Chalukya Empire, soon making himself master of most of the old Rashtrakuta lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Western Chalukya Empire, also called the empire of the Chalukyas of Kalyani or the Later Chalukyas, was founded in 973 by <b>Tailapa II</b>, a Rashtrakuta feudatory who governed lands around Tardavadi in the Bijapur region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vakpati Munja was a poet and patron who fought the Western Chalukya Empire and was captured and executed by <b>Tailapa II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/paramara-dynasty">Paramara dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
